module.exports = [

{ order: 1, title: "What is energy? The nine forms",
content: `
<p>Every chapter in this unit so far has been about what things <em>do</em> &mdash; how forces move objects, how electricity flows. This chapter is about the thing that makes all of that possible. <strong>Energy</strong> is the capacity to make things happen: to move a car, to heat a room, to light a screen, to lift a load. It is not a substance you can hold &mdash; you only ever see what energy <em>does</em> &mdash; but it is the common currency behind every change in the world around you.</p>
<p>Energy is measured in <strong>joules</strong> (J), named after the nineteenth-century physicist James Joule. One joule is a small amount of energy: lifting an apple from the floor to a table uses about 1&nbsp;J, and the chocolate bar in your bag stores about 900&nbsp;000&nbsp;J. Because joules are small, you will often meet <strong>kilojoules</strong> (kJ), where 1&nbsp;kJ = 1000&nbsp;J.</p>
<p>Energy is stored and moved around in nine <strong>forms</strong>, and learning to name them &mdash; and to spot which form is present in a situation &mdash; is the foundation of the whole chapter.</p>
<figure style="margin:1.4rem 0;text-align:center;"><img src="/lesson-assets/sas-physics/sas-phys-energy-forms.png" alt="Nine labelled cards showing the nine forms of energy: chemical, heat, electrical, sound, light, magnetic, strain, kinetic and gravitational potential, each with an icon and an everyday description." style="width:100%;max-width:100%;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.75rem;background:#fff;"><figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem;font-size:0.9rem;color:#475569;text-align:left;">The nine forms of energy. Every device you meet in this chapter can be described as taking one or more of these in and putting others out. Diagram drawn for this course.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Read each card carefully. <strong>Chemical energy</strong> is stored in fuels, food and batteries, waiting to be released. <strong>Heat (thermal) energy</strong> belongs to hot things: the hotter something is, the more its particles vibrate or move, the more heat energy it holds. <strong>Electrical energy</strong> is carried by a current moving through wires. <strong>Sound energy</strong> travels outwards as vibrations of a material &mdash; usually air. <strong>Light energy</strong> is given out by luminous objects like the Sun and lamps. <strong>Magnetic energy</strong> is stored in magnets and electromagnets. <strong>Strain (elastic) energy</strong> is stored in a stretched or squashed object &mdash; a drawn bow, a compressed spring. <strong>Kinetic energy</strong> is the energy anything moving possesses. And <strong>gravitational potential energy</strong> is stored in anything raised up above the ground.</p>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #b45309;background:#fffbeb;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>A common misconception:</strong> the everyday phrase &ldquo;I have no energy today&rdquo; is not the physics meaning. In physics, energy is a measurable quantity that objects and systems possess &mdash; a falling rock has kinetic energy whether or not anyone feels energetic. Energy is not a feeling, a force, or a substance that flows like water; it is a number, in joules, describing a system&rsquo;s capacity for change.</p>
</div>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) Name the form of energy stored in each of the following: a mobile phone battery; a stretched catapult band; a rock resting on a cliff edge; a bowl of hot soup. (b) State the unit of energy and give a meaning for the prefix &ldquo;k&rdquo; when used with it. (c) A passing motorbike makes three different forms of energy. Name all three.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) Phone battery &mdash; chemical; stretched catapult band &mdash; strain (elastic); rock on a cliff edge &mdash; gravitational potential; hot soup &mdash; heat (thermal). (b) The joule (J); the prefix &ldquo;k&rdquo; means thousand, so 1&nbsp;kJ = 1000&nbsp;J. (c) Kinetic energy (it is moving), sound energy (the engine noise) and heat energy (the hot exhaust) &mdash; the engine also stores chemical energy in its fuel, so chemical is a valid fourth answer.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 2, title: "Energy transfers and the conservation of energy",
content: `
<p>Energy never sits still for long. A torch turns stored chemical energy into light. A kettle turns electrical energy into heat. A car engine turns chemical energy into kinetic energy. Each of these changes is an <strong>energy transfer</strong>, and the device is an energy <strong>converter</strong>. Describing what goes in and what comes out is the first skill of this chapter.</p>
<p>The standard way to describe a device is an <strong>energy transfer chain</strong>: the form of energy that goes in (the input), the device, and the form or forms that come out (the outputs). But there is a second, subtler idea hiding inside every chain, and the torch in the figure below shows it perfectly.</p>
<figure style="margin:1.4rem 0;text-align:center;"><img src="/lesson-assets/sas-physics/sas-phys-energy-transfer-chain.png" alt="A flow diagram of a torch: chemical energy stored in the battery flows through the current as electrical energy, then splits into light energy, the useful output, and heat energy, which is wasted and spreads into the air." style="width:100%;max-width:100%;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.75rem;background:#fff;"><figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem;font-size:0.9rem;color:#475569;text-align:left;">Energy transfer in a torch. The current carries the energy from the battery to the lamp, where it splits into the light we want and the heat we do not. The total leaving equals the total that left the battery. Diagram drawn for this course.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Follow the chain: the battery&rsquo;s <strong>chemical energy</strong> is the input; the current carries it to the lamp as <strong>electrical energy</strong>; and the lamp releases two outputs at once &mdash; <strong>light energy</strong>, which is what the torch is <em>for</em>, and <strong>heat energy</strong>, which warms the lamp and the air around it. We call the output a device is designed to produce the <strong>useful</strong> energy, and everything else <strong>wasted</strong> energy. &ldquo;Wasted&rdquo; does not mean destroyed &mdash; it means energy that ends up somewhere we did not want it, usually as heat spreading into the surroundings.</p>
<p>Now the most important law in the whole of physics. When you add up all the outputs &mdash; useful and wasted &mdash; they exactly equal the input. Always. The principle is called the <strong>conservation of energy</strong>:</p>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #1d4ed8;background:#eff6ff;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>Conservation of energy:</strong> energy can be transferred from one form to another, or from one place to another, but it can never be created or destroyed. The total amount of energy stays the same.</p>
</div>
<p>This is why energy is never &ldquo;used up&rdquo;. When your phone battery runs down, the chemical energy it stored has not vanished &mdash; it has been transferred to light and sound from the screen and speaker, and to heat. Every joule the battery lost went somewhere. The reason the phone stops working is that the remaining chemical energy in the battery is too little to keep the circuits running, not because energy disappeared.</p>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) Draw an energy transfer chain for an electric fan, naming the input energy and all output energies. (b) A student says: &ldquo;When a car brakes to a stop, its kinetic energy is destroyed.&rdquo; Explain what actually happens to that energy. (c) A food label states that a cereal bar stores 500&nbsp;kJ. Convert this energy into joules.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) Electrical energy &rarr; fan &rarr; kinetic energy (the spinning blades, useful) + sound energy (the motor noise, wasted) + heat energy (from friction in the motor, wasted). (b) The kinetic energy is transferred, not destroyed: friction between the brake pads and the wheels converts it into heat energy, which spreads into the brakes, wheels and surrounding air, and a little into sound (the squeal). (c) 500&nbsp;kJ = 500&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;1000 = 500&nbsp;000&nbsp;J.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 3, title: "Sankey diagrams: drawing the whole story",
content: `
<p>A written chain tells you the <em>forms</em> of energy involved, but not <em>how much</em> of each. Engineers, and examiners, need a diagram that shows the quantities too &mdash; and the standard tool is the <strong>Sankey diagram</strong>, named after the Irish engineer Matthew Sankey, who used it in 1898 to show the energy flows of a steam engine.</p>
<figure style="margin:1.4rem 0;text-align:center;"><img src="/lesson-assets/sas-physics/sas-phys-sankey-lamp.png" alt="A Sankey diagram for a filament lamp: a wide input arrow representing 100 joules of electrical energy every second splits into a narrow arrow of 10 joules of useful light and a wide arrow of 90 joules of wasted heat." style="width:100%;max-width:100%;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.75rem;background:#fff;"><figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem;font-size:0.9rem;color:#475569;text-align:left;">A Sankey diagram for a filament lamp. The input band splits in proportion: only 10 of every 100 joules become light &mdash; the other 90 become heat. The thickness of each arrow carries the numbers. Diagram drawn for this course.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The rules for reading &mdash; and drawing &mdash; a Sankey diagram are simple but strict. <strong>Energy flows from left to right.</strong> The input is a single arrow on the left. <strong>The thickness of each arrow is proportional to the amount of energy it represents</strong> &mdash; this is the whole point of the diagram, because you can compare flows at a glance. The useful output continues to the right; wasted energy usually branches off <strong>downwards</strong>. In the figure, the lamp takes in 100&nbsp;J of electrical energy every second. Only 10&nbsp;J leaves as light &mdash; drawn as a band one-tenth as thick &mdash; while 90&nbsp;J leaves as heat. Look how wide the heat band is: that is the honest picture of a filament lamp, most of whose energy goes into warming the room rather than lighting it.</p>
<p>To draw one yourself, choose a scale first (for example, 1&nbsp;cm of width represents 10&nbsp;J), draw the input arrow to the correct width, then split it into output arrows whose widths match their quantities. Check your drawing with conservation of energy: the widths of the output bands must add up to the width of the input band.</p>
<h3>Worked example: reading a Sankey diagram</h3>
<div style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.6rem;padding:0.8rem 1.1rem;background:#f8fafc;margin:1.1rem 0;">
<p style="margin:0 0 0.5rem;"><strong>The question.</strong> A Sankey diagram shows an electric motor. The input band is 5&nbsp;cm wide and is labelled &ldquo;500&nbsp;J each second&rdquo;. One output band is 4&nbsp;cm wide and is labelled &ldquo;useful&rdquo;. (a) State the useful energy output. (b) State the wasted energy output. (c) Describe how the diagram would show the wasted energy.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 1 &mdash; find the scale.</strong> The 5&nbsp;cm input band represents 500&nbsp;J, so the scale is 1&nbsp;cm per 100&nbsp;J.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 2 &mdash; apply it to the useful band.</strong> 4&nbsp;cm &times; 100&nbsp;J per cm = 400&nbsp;J useful output each second.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 3 &mdash; use conservation of energy.</strong> Total out must equal total in: wasted = 500&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;400 = 100&nbsp;J each second.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0 0;"><strong>Step 4 &mdash; the drawing convention.</strong> The wasted energy would be shown as a band 1&nbsp;cm wide (representing 100&nbsp;J) branching downwards from the main flow.</p>
</div>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #b45309;background:#fffbeb;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>A common misconception:</strong> the wasted heat in a Sankey diagram has not &ldquo;disappeared&rdquo; from the accounting. It is drawn as a separate arrow precisely because it still exists &mdash; it has been transferred to the surroundings, where it can no longer be put to use. &ldquo;Wasted&rdquo; means unwanted and unusable, not destroyed.</p>
</div>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) A TV takes in 200&nbsp;J of electrical energy each second: 60&nbsp;J leaves as light and sound. Calculate the energy wasted each second, and state the form it takes. (b) Draw, on squared paper, a Sankey diagram for a device with 400&nbsp;J in, 300&nbsp;J useful kinetic output and 100&nbsp;J wasted heat. (c) Explain why the output bands of a correct Sankey diagram must add up to the input band.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) Wasted = 200&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;60 = 140&nbsp;J each second, transferred as heat energy (from the warm casing and circuits). (b) Choose a scale such as 1&nbsp;cm per 100&nbsp;J: input band 4&nbsp;cm wide on the left; useful band 3&nbsp;cm wide continuing right; wasted band 1&nbsp;cm wide branching downwards. (c) Because of conservation of energy: energy cannot be created or destroyed, so the total leaving the device must exactly equal the total entering it; if the widths did not add up, the diagram would break this law.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 4, title: "Kinetic energy: the energy of movement",
content: `
<p>Of the nine forms from page 1, two are so useful that physics gives them their own equations. The first belongs to moving things. Anything that moves &mdash; a car, a football, a dust mote in a sunbeam &mdash; has <strong>kinetic energy</strong>, written E<sub>k</sub>. The amount depends on just two things: how massive the object is, and how fast it is going.</p>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #1d4ed8;background:#eff6ff;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>The equation:</strong> E<sub>k</sub> = &frac12; &times; m &times; v&sup2;, where m is the mass in kilograms (kg), v is the speed in metres per second (m/s), and E<sub>k</sub> is the kinetic energy in joules (J).</p>
</div>
<p>Notice two things about this equation before you use it. First, mass appears just once, but speed is <strong>squared</strong>. That means speed matters far more than mass: doubling the mass of a moving object doubles its kinetic energy, but doubling its speed <em>quadruples</em> it. Second, because v is squared it can never produce a negative energy &mdash; and it does not matter which direction the object is moving, since direction does not enter the equation at all.</p>
<figure style="margin:1.4rem 0;text-align:center;"><img src="/lesson-assets/sas-physics/sas-phys-kinetic-energy.png" alt="The kinetic energy equation shown above two worked examples: a 1000 kg family car travelling at 20 metres per second stores 200 000 joules, and a 60 kg cyclist travelling at 8 metres per second stores 1920 joules." style="width:100%;max-width:100%;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.75rem;background:#fff;"><figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem;font-size:0.9rem;color:#475569;text-align:left;">Kinetic energy for a family car and a cyclist, worked through step by step. Compare the two results: the car stores about a hundred times more kinetic energy than the cyclist, which is why car crashes do so much more damage. Diagram drawn for this course.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Walk through the figure&rsquo;s two calculations. For the car: m = 1000&nbsp;kg and v = 20&nbsp;m/s, so E<sub>k</sub> = &frac12; &times; 1000 &times; 20&sup2; = &frac12; &times; 1000 &times; 400 = 200&nbsp;000&nbsp;J, or 200&nbsp;kJ. For the cyclist: E<sub>k</sub> = &frac12; &times; 60 &times; 8&sup2; = &frac12; &times; 60 &times; 64 = 1920&nbsp;J. And the note underneath the car&rsquo;s result is the speed-squared idea in action: at 40&nbsp;m/s &mdash; double the speed &mdash; the same car stores &frac12; &times; 1000 &times; 1600 = 800&nbsp;000&nbsp;J: four times as much.</p>
<h3>Worked example: using the equation in reverse</h3>
<div style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.6rem;padding:0.8rem 1.1rem;background:#f8fafc;margin:1.1rem 0;">
<p style="margin:0 0 0.5rem;"><strong>The question.</strong> A 65&nbsp;kg runner has 1170&nbsp;J of kinetic energy. Calculate the runner&rsquo;s speed.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 1 &mdash; write the equation and substitute.</strong> E<sub>k</sub> = &frac12;mv&sup2; becomes 1170 = &frac12; &times; 65 &times; v&sup2;.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 2 &mdash; rearrange for v&sup2;.</strong> Multiply both sides by 2: 2340 = 65 &times; v&sup2;. Divide both sides by 65: v&sup2; = 36.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 3 &mdash; take the square root.</strong> v = &radic;36 = 6, so the runner&rsquo;s speed is 6&nbsp;m/s.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0 0;"><strong>Step 4 &mdash; check.</strong> Put it back: &frac12; &times; 65 &times; 6&sup2; = &frac12; &times; 65 &times; 36 = 1170&nbsp;J. It matches.</p>
</div>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) Calculate the kinetic energy of a 70&nbsp;kg cyclist travelling at 10&nbsp;m/s. (b) A 2&nbsp;kg ball has 36&nbsp;J of kinetic energy. Calculate its speed. (c) Explain, using the kinetic energy equation, why motorway speed limits matter so much for road safety.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) E<sub>k</sub> = &frac12; &times; 70 &times; 10&sup2; = &frac12; &times; 70 &times; 100 = 3500&nbsp;J. (b) 36 = &frac12; &times; 2 &times; v&sup2;, so 72 = 2v&sup2;, v&sup2; = 36, v = 6&nbsp;m/s. (c) Because kinetic energy depends on the speed <em>squared</em>, a small increase in speed produces a large increase in kinetic energy: travelling at 30&nbsp;m/s instead of 25&nbsp;m/s raises a car&rsquo;s kinetic energy by 44%, and all of that energy must be transferred away (by braking, into heat) to stop the car &mdash; so faster cars are much harder to stop and crashes do far more damage.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 5, title: "Gravitational potential energy",
content: `
<p>The second special form belongs to raised objects. Lift a book from the floor to a shelf and you do work against gravity; that work is now <em>stored</em> in the book, ready to be given back if the book falls. This is <strong>gravitational potential energy</strong>, written E<sub>p</sub>, and it is the reason a stretched dam of water, a rockslide or a ride at the top of a rollercoaster all carry a hidden store of energy.</p>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #1d4ed8;background:#eff6ff;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>The equation:</strong> E<sub>p</sub> = m &times; g &times; h, where m is the mass in kilograms (kg), g is the gravitational field strength in newtons per kilogram (N/kg), h is the vertical height in metres (m), and E<sub>p</sub> is the gravitational potential energy in joules (J).</p>
</div>
<p>The symbol g measures how strong gravity is where you are. On Earth, g is about 10&nbsp;N/kg, and in CCEA examinations you are given this value and should use it &mdash; so every calculation in this chapter uses g = 10&nbsp;N/kg. The value of h must be the <strong>vertical height</strong>: the height of the object above whatever level you have chosen to measure from, which is why you should always <em>state</em> your starting level before calculating.</p>
<figure style="margin:1.4rem 0;text-align:center;"><img src="/lesson-assets/sas-physics/sas-phys-gpe.png" alt="A labelled diagram of a 500 kilogram load lifted by 12 metres onto a platform, with a height arrow marked h equals 12 metres from the ground level, alongside a worked calculation of its gravitational potential energy of 60 000 joules." style="width:100%;max-width:100%;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.75rem;background:#fff;"><figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem;font-size:0.9rem;color:#475569;text-align:left;">Gravitational potential energy stored in a lifted load. The height h is measured vertically from ground level &mdash; the stated starting level. Diagram drawn for this course.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The figure works through a complete example: a 500&nbsp;kg load sitting 12&nbsp;m above ground level stores E<sub>p</sub> = 500 &times; 10 &times; 12 = 60&nbsp;000&nbsp;J, or 60&nbsp;kJ. Every one of those joules had to be supplied by the machine that lifted the load &mdash; and every one is available to be released if the load falls.</p>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<div style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.6rem;padding:0.8rem 1.1rem;background:#f8fafc;margin:1.1rem 0;">
<p style="margin:0 0 0.5rem;"><strong>The question.</strong> A crane lifts a 250&nbsp;kg beam through a vertical height of 15&nbsp;m. Calculate the gain in gravitational potential energy.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 1 &mdash; list the quantities.</strong> m = 250&nbsp;kg, g = 10&nbsp;N/kg, h = 15&nbsp;m.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 2 &mdash; substitute into the equation.</strong> E<sub>p</sub> = 250 &times; 10 &times; 15 = 37&nbsp;500&nbsp;J.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0 0;"><strong>Step 3 &mdash; check the units.</strong> kg &times; N/kg &times; m multiplies out to newtons &times; metres = joules, so 37&nbsp;500&nbsp;J (about 38&nbsp;kJ) is a sensible answer for a quarter-tonne beam.</p>
</div>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #b45309;background:#fffbeb;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>A common misconception:</strong> h is the <em>vertical</em> height the object is raised, not the distance it travels. A skier gliding 200&nbsp;m down a slope that drops only 40&nbsp;m gains the same gravitational potential energy (mgh with h = 40&nbsp;m) as one lowered straight down 40&nbsp;m &mdash; the long diagonal route stores no extra energy against gravity.</p>
</div>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) Calculate the gravitational potential energy of a 40&nbsp;kg child at the top of a 5&nbsp;m slide. (b) A climber of mass 55&nbsp;kg gains 11&nbsp;000&nbsp;J of gravitational potential energy. Calculate the vertical height climbed. (c) A tray of books of mass 8&nbsp;kg is carried along a flat corridor for 20&nbsp;m. Explain why its gravitational potential energy does not change.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) E<sub>p</sub> = 40 &times; 10 &times; 5 = 2000&nbsp;J. (b) Rearrange: h = E<sub>p</sub> &divide; (m &times; g) = 11&nbsp;000 &divide; (55 &times; 10) = 20&nbsp;m. (c) Gravitational potential energy depends on vertical height above the chosen level; on a flat corridor the height does not change (h = 0), so no energy is stored against gravity &mdash; the carrying force does no work against gravity, only against friction in practice.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 6, title: "Falling objects: energy changing form",
content: `
<p>Here is where the two equations from the last two pages meet &mdash; and where the conservation of energy from page 2 becomes something you can <em>calculate</em> with. Hold an object up and it stores gravitational potential energy. Let go, and as it falls, it loses height and gains speed: gravitational potential energy is being transferred into kinetic energy, joule by joule, the whole way down.</p>
<figure style="margin:1.4rem 0;text-align:center;"><img src="/lesson-assets/sas-physics/sas-phys-falling-conversion.png" alt="A falling 10 kilogram ball shown at the start of a 20 metre drop, halfway down and just before landing, next to bar charts showing gravitational potential energy of 2000 joules converting into kinetic energy so that the total stays 2000 joules at every point of the fall." style="width:100%;max-width:100%;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.75rem;background:#fff;"><figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem;font-size:0.9rem;color:#475569;text-align:left;">A 10&nbsp;kg ball dropped from 20&nbsp;m (g = 10&nbsp;N/kg). At the start it stores 2000&nbsp;J of gravitational potential energy; halfway down half has converted to kinetic; just before landing all 2000&nbsp;J is kinetic. The total never changes. Diagram drawn for this course.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Trace the figure&rsquo;s numbers. Before release: E<sub>p</sub> = 10 &times; 10 &times; 20 = 2000&nbsp;J, E<sub>k</sub> = 0. Halfway down, at h = 10&nbsp;m: E<sub>p</sub> = 10 &times; 10 &times; 10 = 1000&nbsp;J, and the other 1000&nbsp;J has become kinetic energy &mdash; the ball&rsquo;s speed there works out at about 14&nbsp;m/s. Just before landing: E<sub>p</sub> = 0, E<sub>k</sub> = 2000&nbsp;J. At every point of the fall, <strong>E<sub>p</sub> + E<sub>k</sub> = 2000&nbsp;J</strong>. This is conservation of energy as a working calculation, and it is one of the most examined ideas in this unit.</p>
<p>Two honest qualifications. First, this bookkeeping assumes <strong>no air resistance</strong> &mdash; the classic examination assumption. In reality, a falling object pushes air out of the way, transferring a little energy to the air as heat and sound, so a real ball arrives moving slightly slower than the ideal calculation predicts. Second, the ball&rsquo;s <em>mass</em> never appears in the final answer for speed: a heavier ball dropped from the same height gains more E<sub>k</sub>, but it also needs more energy to reach a given speed, and the two effects exactly cancel.</p>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #b45309;background:#fffbeb;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>A common misconception:</strong> &ldquo;Heavier objects fall faster.&rdquo; They do not. In the absence of air resistance, every object falls with the same acceleration regardless of mass &mdash; a hammer and a feather released together land together, as astronauts demonstrated on the Moon in 1971. Everyday experience suggests otherwise only because air resistance slows a feather more than a hammer.</p>
</div>
<h3>Worked example: finding the landing speed</h3>
<div style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.6rem;padding:0.8rem 1.1rem;background:#f8fafc;margin:1.1rem 0;">
<p style="margin:0 0 0.5rem;"><strong>The question.</strong> A 0.5&nbsp;kg apple falls from a branch 3.2&nbsp;m above the ground. Ignoring air resistance, calculate its speed just before it lands.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 1 &mdash; energy at the top.</strong> E<sub>p</sub> = 0.5 &times; 10 &times; 3.2 = 16&nbsp;J; E<sub>k</sub> = 0.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 2 &mdash; transfer all of it.</strong> Just before landing all 16&nbsp;J has become kinetic energy (no air resistance), so E<sub>k</sub> = 16&nbsp;J.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 3 &mdash; use the kinetic equation.</strong> 16 = &frac12; &times; 0.5 &times; v&sup2;, so 32 = 0.5v&sup2;, v&sup2; = 64, v = 8&nbsp;m/s.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0 0;"><strong>Step 4 &mdash; notice the shortcut.</strong> The apple&rsquo;s mass cancelled out &mdash; any object falling 3.2&nbsp;m lands at 8&nbsp;m/s under this assumption.</p>
</div>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) A 60&nbsp;kg parachutist&rsquo;s jump (before the parachute opens) starts 900&nbsp;m up; ignore air resistance and calculate her kinetic energy just before landing. (b) Use your answer to calculate her landing speed, and comment on how realistic the assumption of no air resistance is here. (c) A ball thrown straight up has 45&nbsp;J of kinetic energy as it leaves a hand of negligible height. State its gravitational potential energy at the top of its flight, and explain your reasoning.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) E<sub>p</sub> at the start = 60 &times; 10 &times; 900 = 540&nbsp;000&nbsp;J, all of which becomes kinetic energy, so E<sub>k</sub> = 540&nbsp;000&nbsp;J (540&nbsp;kJ). (b) 540&nbsp;000 = &frac12; &times; 60 &times; v&sup2; gives v&sup2; = 18&nbsp;000, v = 134&nbsp;m/s (about 480&nbsp;km/h). Very unrealistic: air resistance on a falling person is enormous, and it transfers much of the energy to the air as heat long before the ground, which is exactly why parachutes work. (c) 45&nbsp;J. Rising, kinetic energy converts to gravitational potential energy; at the top the ball is momentarily at rest (E<sub>k</sub> = 0), so by conservation of energy all 45&nbsp;J is stored as E<sub>p</sub> (ignoring air resistance).</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 7, title: "Calculations corner: E<sub>k</sub> and E<sub>p</sub> together",
content: `
<p>The last three pages have built a complete toolkit: the kinetic energy equation, the gravitational potential energy equation, and the law that connects them. This page is deliberately nothing but practice in combining all three &mdash; the skill that the highest-mark questions in this topic test. In every problem, the plan is the same: work out one form of energy at a position where it is easy to find, then let conservation of energy carry the number across to where the question wants it.</p>
<h3>Worked example 1: a pendulum</h3>
<div style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.6rem;padding:0.8rem 1.1rem;background:#f8fafc;margin:1.1rem 0;">
<p style="margin:0 0 0.5rem;"><strong>The question.</strong> The 0.4&nbsp;kg bob of a pendulum is pulled aside until it is 0.5&nbsp;m above the lowest point of its swing. Ignoring air resistance, calculate its maximum speed at the bottom of the swing.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 1 &mdash; energy at the top of the swing.</strong> E<sub>p</sub> = 0.4 &times; 10 &times; 0.5 = 2&nbsp;J. At that moment the bob is momentarily still, so E<sub>k</sub> = 0.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 2 &mdash; energy at the bottom.</strong> The lowest point is the height we measure from, so there E<sub>p</sub> = 0 and, ignoring air resistance, all 2&nbsp;J is kinetic energy.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 3 &mdash; solve for speed.</strong> 2 = &frac12; &times; 0.4 &times; v&sup2; gives v&sup2; = 10, so v = 3.2&nbsp;m/s (rounded to two significant figures).</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0 0;"><strong>Step 4 &mdash; check the meaning.</strong> &ldquo;Maximum&rdquo; speed is at the bottom because that is where the greatest E<sub>p</sub> has been converted; on the far side of the swing the kinetic energy converts back to E<sub>p</sub> and the bob slows to rest.</p>
</div>
<h3>Worked example 2: a skier</h3>
<div style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.6rem;padding:0.8rem 1.1rem;background:#f8fafc;margin:1.1rem 0;">
<p style="margin:0 0 0.5rem;"><strong>The question.</strong> A 60&nbsp;kg skier starts from rest at the top of a 40&nbsp;m slope and glides straight down. Ignoring friction and air resistance, calculate her speed at the bottom.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 1 &mdash; energy at the top.</strong> E<sub>p</sub> = 60 &times; 10 &times; 40 = 24&nbsp;000&nbsp;J (the length of the slope does not matter &mdash; only the vertical drop).</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 2 &mdash; energy at the bottom.</strong> All 24&nbsp;000&nbsp;J is now kinetic energy.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 3 &mdash; solve.</strong> 24&nbsp;000 = &frac12; &times; 60 &times; v&sup2; gives v&sup2; = 800, so v = 28&nbsp;m/s (two significant figures).</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0 0;"><strong>Step 4 &mdash; test the assumption.</strong> Real snow has friction and real air has resistance, so a real skier is slower &mdash; friction transfers some of the energy straight to heat on the way down. The ideal answer is an upper limit.</p>
</div>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #1d4ed8;background:#eff6ff;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>The key idea:</strong> in these problems, energy is the bridge between two moments in time. You rarely need the forces or the route &mdash; only the starting height, the ending height, and the law that the total stays constant.</p>
</div>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) A 20&nbsp;kg sledge released from rest at the top of an icy 5&nbsp;m slope reaches the bottom. Ignoring friction, calculate its kinetic energy and its speed there. (b) A tennis ball of mass 0.06&nbsp;kg is thrown vertically upwards with a speed of 15&nbsp;m/s. Ignoring air resistance, calculate the maximum height it reaches. (c) The ball in (b) actually rises to about 9&nbsp;m rather than your calculated value. Explain the difference, naming the energy transfers involved.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) E<sub>p</sub> at top = 20 &times; 10 &times; 5 = 1000&nbsp;J, so E<sub>k</sub> = 1000&nbsp;J at the bottom; 1000 = &frac12; &times; 20 &times; v&sup2; gives v&sup2; = 100, v = 10&nbsp;m/s. (b) Kinetic energy at launch = &frac12; &times; 0.06 &times; 15&sup2; = 6.75&nbsp;J; at the top all of this is E<sub>p</sub>, so 6.75 = 0.06 &times; 10 &times; h, giving h = 11.25&nbsp;m (about 11&nbsp;m). (c) Air resistance acts throughout the flight, transferring some of the ball&rsquo;s kinetic energy to heat and sound in the surrounding air as it moves &mdash; so less energy remains to be stored as gravitational potential energy at the top, and the maximum height is lower than the ideal prediction.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 8, title: "Power: how quickly energy is transferred",
content: `
<p>Two kettles can each boil the same amount of water using the same total energy &mdash; but if one takes a minute and the other takes five, they are very different machines. Total energy tells only half the story; the other half is <strong>rate</strong>. <strong>Power</strong> is the rate at which energy is transferred &mdash; how many joules per second a device moves.</p>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #1d4ed8;background:#eff6ff;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>The equation:</strong> P = E &divide; t, where E is the energy transferred in joules (J), t is the time taken in seconds (s), and P is the power in watts (W). One watt means one joule transferred every second: 1&nbsp;W = 1&nbsp;J/s.</p>
</div>
<p>Because household devices transfer thousands of joules each second, their power ratings are often quoted in <strong>kilowatts</strong> (kW), where 1&nbsp;kW = 1000&nbsp;W. The label on any appliance &mdash; a kettle marked 2.2&nbsp;kW, a television marked 100&nbsp;W &mdash; is telling you exactly this: how fast the device transfers energy when it is running.</p>
<figure style="margin:1.4rem 0;text-align:center;"><img src="/lesson-assets/sas-physics/sas-phys-power.png" alt="The power equation shown above three example cards, a 2200 watt kettle, a 100 watt television and an 8 watt LED lamp, and a worked calculation that a 2200 watt kettle transfers 132 000 joules in 60 seconds." style="width:100%;max-width:100%;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.75rem;background:#fff;"><figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem;font-size:0.9rem;color:#475569;text-align:left;">Power ratings in context. A kettle transfers 2200 joules every second; an LED lamp just 8. The worked example rearranges the equation to find the total energy a kettle transfers in a minute. Diagram drawn for this course.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The figure&rsquo;s calculation shows the equation used in reverse: a 2200&nbsp;W kettle running for 60&nbsp;s transfers E = P &times; t = 2200 &times; 60 = 132&nbsp;000&nbsp;J, or 132&nbsp;kJ. Whenever you know two of power, energy and time, the third follows &mdash; and whenever a question mixes power with the energy forms from earlier pages, treat power as the bridge between them.</p>
<h3>Worked example: human power</h3>
<div style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.6rem;padding:0.8rem 1.1rem;background:#f8fafc;margin:1.1rem 0;">
<p style="margin:0 0 0.5rem;"><strong>The question.</strong> A student of mass 50&nbsp;kg runs up a staircase 4&nbsp;m high in 5&nbsp;s. Calculate the useful power she develops.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 1 &mdash; find the energy transferred.</strong> Lifting her body against gravity stores gravitational potential energy: E<sub>p</sub> = 50 &times; 10 &times; 4 = 2000&nbsp;J.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 2 &mdash; divide by the time.</strong> P = E &divide; t = 2000 &divide; 5 = 400&nbsp;W.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0 0;"><strong>Step 3 &mdash; sanity-check.</strong> 400&nbsp;W is about a fifth of a kettle &mdash; plausible for a short burst of hard effort, and a neat reminder that humans are far less powerful than the machines around them.</p>
</div>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #b45309;background:#fffbeb;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>A common misconception:</strong> power is not the same as energy. A 3&nbsp;kW heater is more <em>powerful</em> than a 60&nbsp;W lamp, but left running for different lengths of time they can transfer exactly the same <em>total</em> energy &mdash; the lamp simply needs fifty times longer to do it. &ldquo;Powerful&rdquo; describes the rate, never the amount.</p>
</div>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) A hairdryer transfers 66&nbsp;000&nbsp;J in 60&nbsp;s. Calculate its power. (b) A 500&nbsp;W computer monitor is left on for 2 hours. Calculate the energy transferred, in kilojoules. (c) Two motors each lift the same 100&nbsp;kg load through 6&nbsp;m; motor A takes 10&nbsp;s and motor B takes 40&nbsp;s. Calculate the useful power of each, and state which motor is more powerful.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) P = 66&nbsp;000 &divide; 60 = 1100&nbsp;W (1.1&nbsp;kW). (b) t = 2 &times; 3600 = 7200&nbsp;s; E = P &times; t = 500 &times; 7200 = 3&nbsp;600&nbsp;000&nbsp;J = 3600&nbsp;kJ. (c) Both motors transfer the same useful energy: E<sub>p</sub> = 100 &times; 10 &times; 6 = 6000&nbsp;J. Motor A: P = 6000 &divide; 10 = 600&nbsp;W; motor B: P = 6000 &divide; 40 = 150&nbsp;W. Motor A is more powerful &mdash; four times the rate for the same energy delivered.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 9, title: "Where our energy comes from",
content: `<p>Everything in this chapter so far has been about energy <em>changing form</em>. This page asks the question behind it all: where do we get the energy in the first place? Almost every joule that moves through your home, your food and your transport can be traced back to a small list of <strong>energy resources</strong> &mdash; natural stores that human technology can tap.</p>
<p>The single most important division among them is how long they will last. A <strong>renewable</strong> resource is one that will not run out on a human timescale: the Sun keeps shining, the wind keeps blowing, water keeps flowing through the water cycle. A <strong>non-renewable</strong> resource exists as a fixed stock in the Earth&rsquo;s crust: once it is extracted and used, that stock is gone forever.</p>
<figure style="margin:1.4rem 0;text-align:center;"><img src="/lesson-assets/sas-physics/sas-phys-energy-resources.png" alt="Two labelled panels: renewable resources on the left, wind, solar, hydroelectric and wave, each with an icon and a short description, and non-renewable resources on the right, coal, oil, natural gas and nuclear, each with an icon and a short description." style="width:100%;max-width:100%;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.75rem;background:#fff;"><figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem;font-size:0.9rem;color:#475569;text-align:left;">The main energy resources, sorted by whether they will run out. Fossil fuels took millions of years to form &mdash; we are using them far faster than they can ever be replaced. Diagram drawn for this course.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The <strong>non-renewables</strong> divide into two families. The three <strong>fossil fuels</strong> &mdash; coal, oil and natural gas &mdash; are the compressed remains of organisms that lived millions of years ago, crushed and heated underground into concentrated chemical energy. They are burned in power stations, vehicles and boilers, releasing chemical energy as heat, which is used to generate electricity. <strong>Nuclear fuel</strong> (usually uranium) is different: its energy does not come from burning but from splitting uranium atoms in a reactor, which releases heat directly. Uranium is a fixed stock in the Earth, so it is non-renewable too.</p>
<p>The <strong>renewables</strong> share one feature: their energy mostly originates from the Sun. Wind is air set in motion by the Sun&rsquo;s uneven heating of the Earth; waves are wind over the sea; hydroelectric power is water raised by evaporation and rainfall, lifted by solar heat and returning downhill through rivers. Only two renewable stores are independent of the Sun in any direct sense: <strong>tidal power</strong>, driven by the pull of the Moon on the oceans, and <strong>geothermal</strong> energy, which is heat rising from the Earth&rsquo;s own interior. <strong>Biomass</strong> &mdash; wood and crops grown to be burned &mdash; counts as renewable only if it is replanted as fast as it is used.</p>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #1d4ed8;background:#eff6ff;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>The key idea:</strong> electricity generation is almost always the same story &mdash; get something to turn. Fossil, nuclear and biomass stations make steam to spin a turbine; wind and tidal flow move blades directly; falling water spins a turbine. The generator, turning inside a magnetic field, converts that kinetic energy into electrical energy for the grid.</p>
</div>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) Copy the list and label each resource renewable or non-renewable: wind; coal; tidal; natural gas; geothermal; oil; biomass. (b) Explain why biomass is described as renewable, while coal &mdash; also made from once-living material &mdash; is not. (c) Describe how a fossil-fuel power station turns chemical energy into electrical energy, naming every energy form involved in order.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) Wind &mdash; renewable; coal &mdash; non-renewable; tidal &mdash; renewable; natural gas &mdash; non-renewable; geothermal &mdash; renewable; oil &mdash; non-renewable; biomass &mdash; renewable. (b) Biomass can be replanted and regrown on a timescale of months to years, so the store can be replaced as fast as it is used; coal took millions of years to form, so it cannot be replaced on any human timescale &mdash; it is a one-off stock. (c) Chemical energy in the fuel is released by burning as heat energy; the heat boils water into steam, whose kinetic energy spins a turbine; the turbine turns a generator inside a magnetic field, producing electrical energy (with heat wasted to the surroundings at every stage).</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 10, title: "Choosing between resources: the trade-offs",
content: `
<p>If renewables never run out, why does the world still use fossil fuels for the majority of its energy? Because &ldquo;renewable&rdquo; is only one of several judgement criteria, and every resource &mdash; renewable and non-renewable alike &mdash; has weaknesses. Choosing an energy resource is a decision about trade-offs, and it is a classic examination question precisely because there is no single right answer.</p>
<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:0.95rem;margin:1.2rem 0;">
<thead><tr><th style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;text-align:left;background:#f1f5f9;">Resource</th><th style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;text-align:left;background:#f1f5f9;">Main advantages</th><th style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;text-align:left;background:#f1f5f9;">Main disadvantages</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;"><strong>Coal, oil, gas</strong></td><td style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;">concentrated chemical energy; reliable &mdash; can generate day and night; the technology and infrastructure already exist</td><td style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;">will run out; burning releases carbon dioxide (a greenhouse gas) and other pollutants; extraction damages landscapes and risks spills</td></tr>
<tr><td style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;"><strong>Nuclear</strong></td><td style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;">enormous energy from very little fuel; no carbon dioxide released while generating; reliable continuous output</td><td style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;">uranium is non-renewable; radioactive waste must be stored safely for thousands of years; accidents are rare but severe; plants are very expensive to build and to decommission</td></tr>
<tr><td style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;"><strong>Wind</strong></td><td style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;">never runs out; no fuel and no greenhouse gases while generating; farmland beneath turbines can still be farmed</td><td style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;">intermittent &mdash; no generation when the wind drops; some consider turbines noisy and visually intrusive; best sites are often far from cities</td></tr>
<tr><td style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;"><strong>Solar</strong></td><td style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;">never runs out; no greenhouse gases while generating; can be installed on existing roofs</td><td style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;">intermittent &mdash; no generation at night and little in cloud; output varies with weather and season; large farms compete for land</td></tr>
<tr><td style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;"><strong>Hydroelectric</strong></td><td style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;">never runs out; very reliable and can respond to demand within minutes; reservoirs can also manage floods and water supply</td><td style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;">suitable valleys are limited; damming floods farmland and displaces wildlife and communities; large construction cost</td></tr>
<tr><td style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;"><strong>Wave / tidal</strong></td><td style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;">never runs out; tidal power is entirely predictable (unlike wind or sun); no greenhouse gases while generating</td><td style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;">machines must survive storms and salt water; installation and maintenance are difficult and expensive; generation is limited to coasts</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Read the table with three questions in mind, because these are the axes on which every resource is judged. <strong>Reliability:</strong> can it generate on demand, or only when the weather allows? <strong>Environment:</strong> what does it release, flood, dig up or store away? <strong>Cost:</strong> what does it cost to build, run and connect to the grid? Notice that no column is all good or all bad &mdash; which is why real energy policy is a mixture: renewables growing, nuclear providing steady output, and fossil fuels still filling the gaps, with their carbon dioxide emissions being the main reason the mixture is shifting.</p>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) Give two reasons why a country might build a nuclear power station rather than a wind farm of the same power output. (b) Explain why a country with an energy mix of wind, hydroelectric and solar still needs some form of reliable back-up generation. (c) &ldquo;Renewable resources cause no environmental damage.&rdquo; Comment on this statement, giving an example for and against.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) Any two: nuclear stations generate continuously, day and night, whatever the weather, while wind generation stops when the wind drops; nuclear plants occupy far less land for the same power output; nuclear releases no carbon dioxide while generating; a wind farm large enough to match a nuclear station would need vast areas of countryside. (b) Wind and solar are intermittent &mdash; they generate only when the weather cooperates &mdash; so on a still, overcast day output could fall well below demand; a reliable back-up (hydroelectric, nuclear or a gas station able to start quickly) is needed to guarantee supply at all times. (c) The statement is too strong. Against it: wind turbines change the landscape and can kill birds; hydroelectric dams flood valleys, destroying habitats and displacing communities. For it: once built, renewables release no carbon dioxide or air pollution during generation, unlike fossil fuels &mdash; so their environmental damage is far smaller, but not zero.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 11, title: "Efficiency: useful energy out of total energy in",
content: `
<p>Page 2 divided every device&rsquo;s output into useful energy and wasted energy. Efficiency is the number that measures how good a device is at telling the two apart.</p>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #1d4ed8;background:#eff6ff;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>The equation:</strong> efficiency = (useful energy transferred &divide; total energy supplied) &times; 100&nbsp;%. It is always a percentage, because it compares part of the energy with all of it.</p>
</div>
<figure style="margin:1.4rem 0;text-align:center;"><img src="/lesson-assets/sas-physics/sas-phys-efficiency.png" alt="A motor with 200 joules of electrical energy in producing 120 joules of useful kinetic energy and 80 joules of wasted heat and sound, above a worked efficiency calculation of 60 percent." style="width:100%;max-width:100%;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.75rem;background:#fff;"><figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem;font-size:0.9rem;color:#475569;text-align:left;">Efficiency of an electric motor: 120&nbsp;J useful out of 200&nbsp;J in gives (120 &divide; 200) &times; 100&nbsp;% = 60&nbsp;%. The remaining 80&nbsp;J is not destroyed &mdash; it is transferred to heat and sound. Diagram drawn for this course.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Walk through the figure&rsquo;s numbers, because every efficiency calculation follows exactly this shape. The motor takes in 200&nbsp;J of electrical energy. Of that, 120&nbsp;J becomes kinetic energy &mdash; the useful output &mdash; and 80&nbsp;J is transferred to heat (friction in the bearings and the windings) and sound (the hum). Efficiency = (120 &divide; 200) &times; 100&nbsp;% = 60&nbsp;%. The conservation law from page 2 is doing silent work here: you can find the wasted energy by subtraction because you know nothing vanished.</p>
<p>Efficiency gives you a quick way to compare devices that do the same job. An old filament lamp converts about 10&nbsp;% of its energy to light; an LED lamp converts around 90&nbsp;% &mdash; which is why swapping filament lamps for LEDs cuts the electricity used for lighting so dramatically. A car engine manages only about 25&ndash;30&nbsp;%, most of the fuel&rsquo;s chemical energy leaving the exhaust and radiator as heat. Power stations sit near 35&ndash;45&nbsp;% for the same reason: boiling water and spinning turbines inevitably leaks energy to the surroundings.</p>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #b45309;background:#fffbeb;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>A common misconception:</strong> no device can be more than 100&nbsp;% efficient. An answer above 100&nbsp;% is not an impressive machine &mdash; it is a calculation error, because the useful output can never exceed the total input (that would require creating energy from nothing). The best any device can ever manage is 100&nbsp;%, and in the real world, where friction and heat loss are unavoidable, nothing reaches it.</p>
</div>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) A washing machine takes in 240&nbsp;kJ of electrical energy and delivers 96&nbsp;kJ of useful output. Calculate its efficiency. (b) A lift motor has an efficiency of 75&nbsp;%. Calculate the useful energy it delivers from 800&nbsp;J of electrical input. (c) A device is supplied with 500&nbsp;J and is 20&nbsp;% efficient. Calculate the energy wasted, and state two likely forms the wasted energy takes.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) (96 &divide; 240) &times; 100&nbsp;% = 40&nbsp;%. (b) Useful energy = 75&nbsp;% of 800&nbsp;J = 0.75 &times; 800 = 600&nbsp;J. (c) Useful energy = 20&nbsp;% of 500&nbsp;J = 100&nbsp;J, so wasted energy = 500&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;100 = 400&nbsp;J, most likely as heat (friction in moving parts, warm circuits) and sound (motor and vibration noise).</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 12, title: "Efficiency calculations and how to improve it",
content: `
<p>This page adds two further skills to efficiency: using the equation with power instead of energy, and reasoning about <em>how</em> a device&rsquo;s efficiency could be improved. Both appear regularly in examination questions on this topic.</p>
<p>First, a convenience worth noticing. Efficiency works equally well with <strong>power</strong> as with energy &mdash; useful power out divided by total power in &mdash; because power is just energy per second, and the seconds cancel. A motor drawing 1500&nbsp;W of electrical power and delivering 900&nbsp;W of useful mechanical power is (900 &divide; 1500) &times; 100&nbsp;% = 60&nbsp;% efficient, no time values needed.</p>
<h3>Worked example: a kettle, in full</h3>
<div style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.6rem;padding:0.8rem 1.1rem;background:#f8fafc;margin:1.1rem 0;">
<p style="margin:0 0 0.5rem;"><strong>The question.</strong> A 3000&nbsp;W kettle runs for 120&nbsp;s, drawing 360&nbsp;000&nbsp;J of electrical energy. Of this, 252&nbsp;000&nbsp;J actually heats the water. (a) Calculate the efficiency. (b) Explain where the remaining energy went. (c) Suggest two changes that would improve the efficiency.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 1 &mdash; efficiency.</strong> (252&nbsp;000 &divide; 360&nbsp;000) &times; 100&nbsp;% = 70&nbsp;%.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 2 &mdash; account for the rest.</strong> The remaining 108&nbsp;000&nbsp;J was transferred to heat energy in the kettle&rsquo;s own body and surroundings (warming the plastic casing, the element mountings and the air around the kettle) and a little to sound (the rumbling noise) &mdash; energy that is wasted because it never reaches the water.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0 0;"><strong>Step 3 &mdash; improvements.</strong> Insulate the kettle&rsquo;s walls (less heat conducted to the casing and air) and boil only the amount of water needed (less thermal mass to heat in the first place). A flat-bottomed element in close contact with the water would also cut losses.</p>
</div>
<p>Improving efficiency always means hunting down the wasted energy and reducing it at its source. The three great enemies are friction, air resistance and unwanted heat loss, and each has standard countermeasures: <strong>lubricate</strong> moving parts so less kinetic energy converts to heat through friction; <strong>streamline</strong> shapes so less energy is spent pushing air out of the way; <strong>insulate</strong> hot parts so heat stays where it is useful. Devices whose wasted energy is mainly heat in circuits &mdash; like filament lamps &mdash; can be replaced by designs that waste less in the first place, such as LEDs, which make light a completely different way.</p>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #1d4ed8;background:#eff6ff;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>The key idea:</strong> improving efficiency does not change how much useful work a device does &mdash; it changes how much <em>input</em> energy is needed to do it. That is why efficiency matters beyond the physics examination: a more efficient kettle, car or power station delivers the same useful output while extracting and burning less fuel, saving money and cutting carbon dioxide emissions.</p>
</div>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) An electric motor draws 2500&nbsp;W and delivers 2000&nbsp;W of useful mechanical power. Calculate its efficiency, then state the wasted power and its most likely form. (b) A filament lamp is 10&nbsp;% efficient and an LED lamp is 90&nbsp;% efficient. Explain what these figures mean for the energy needed to produce the same useful light output. (c) A cyclist pedals steadily; her body is about 25&nbsp;% efficient at turning chemical energy from food into useful mechanical work. State the main way the other 75&nbsp;% leaves her body, and suggest why a racing cyclist adopts a crouched position.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) Efficiency = (2000 &divide; 2500) &times; 100&nbsp;% = 80&nbsp;%; wasted power = 500&nbsp;W, transferred mostly as heat from friction in the bearings and electrical resistance in the windings, plus some sound. (b) Only 10&nbsp;% of a filament lamp&rsquo;s input becomes light, so 90&nbsp;% is wasted as heat; an LED converts 90&nbsp;% to light, so it delivers the same useful light with about one-ninth of the electrical energy input (and stays cool rather than heating the room). (c) The other 75&nbsp;% leaves mainly as heat energy from the body (the cyclist gets hot and sweats to shed it); a crouched, streamlined position reduces air resistance, so less of her useful mechanical work is spent pushing air aside, wasting less energy at a given speed.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 13, title: "Putting it together: an examination-style question",
content: `
<p>This page is a full multi-part question, worked through completely &mdash; the kind that combines several ideas from the chapter and carries the highest marks. Read the worked solution for its <em>structure</em> as much as its numbers: each part is answered in the same deliberate steps you have practised on the earlier pages.</p>
<h3>Worked example: a goods lift</h3>
<div style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.6rem;padding:0.8rem 1.1rem;background:#f8fafc;margin:1.1rem 0;">
<p style="margin:0 0 0.5rem;"><strong>The question.</strong> A goods lift uses an electric motor to raise a 300&nbsp;kg crate through a vertical height of 6&nbsp;m. The motor&rsquo;s input power is 2000&nbsp;W and it runs for 15&nbsp;s. (g = 10&nbsp;N/kg)</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 0.5rem;">(a) Calculate the gravitational potential energy gained by the crate.</p>
<p style="margin:0.2rem 0 0.5rem;"><strong>Step 1 &mdash; equation and substitution.</strong> E<sub>p</sub> = mgh = 300 &times; 10 &times; 6 = 18&nbsp;000&nbsp;J. The crate&rsquo;s height is measured from its starting level, as established on page 5.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0 0.5rem;">(b) Calculate the electrical energy supplied to the motor.</p>
<p style="margin:0.2rem 0 0.5rem;"><strong>Step 2 &mdash; power and time.</strong> E = P &times; t = 2000 &times; 15 = 30&nbsp;000&nbsp;J. Note the unit discipline: seconds, so no conversion is needed.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0 0.5rem;">(c) Calculate the efficiency of the lift system.</p>
<p style="margin:0.2rem 0 0.5rem;"><strong>Step 3 &mdash; identify useful and total.</strong> The useful energy is what ended up stored in the raised crate: 18&nbsp;000&nbsp;J. The total supplied is the motor&rsquo;s input: 30&nbsp;000&nbsp;J. Efficiency = (18&nbsp;000 &divide; 30&nbsp;000) &times; 100&nbsp;% = 60&nbsp;%.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0 0.5rem;">(d) State what happened to the remaining 12&nbsp;000&nbsp;J, and suggest one way to improve the system&rsquo;s efficiency.</p>
<p style="margin:0.2rem 0 0.5rem;"><strong>Step 4 &mdash; conservation, then improvement.</strong> The remaining 12&nbsp;000&nbsp;J was not destroyed: it was transferred to heat energy (friction in the motor, gearbox, guides and cables &mdash; everything warms slightly) and to sound, and finally spread into the surroundings. Improvements target those losses: lubricating and maintaining the moving parts to cut friction, or a better-matched motor so it does not waste energy idling.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0 0;"><strong>How the marks work.</strong> Each part is marked for the correct method (the equation chosen), the substitution, and the final answer with units &mdash; so even if arithmetic slips, the method earns credit. In (d), marks are for naming the <em>forms</em> of the wasted energy and the conservation reasoning, not just the number.</p>
</div>
<h3>Exam-style practice</h3>
<p>A pump is rated at 800&nbsp;W and runs for 25&nbsp;s, drawing electrical energy from the mains. It lifts 120&nbsp;kg of water through a vertical height of 12&nbsp;m. (g = 10&nbsp;N/kg)<br/>(a) Calculate the electrical energy supplied to the pump. (b) Calculate the gravitational potential energy gained by the water. (c) Calculate the pump&rsquo;s efficiency. (d) The pump&rsquo;s wasted energy is transferred mainly to heat and sound. State <em>two</em> places in the system where that heating happens, and explain how the wasted energy eventually reaches the surroundings.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) E = P &times; t = 800 &times; 25 = 20&nbsp;000&nbsp;J. (b) E<sub>p</sub> = mgh = 120 &times; 10 &times; 12 = 14&nbsp;400&nbsp;J. (c) Efficiency = (14&nbsp;400 &divide; 20&nbsp;000) &times; 100&nbsp;% = 72&nbsp;%. (d) Heating happens in the pump&rsquo;s motor (electrical resistance in the windings and friction in the bearings) and in the pipes and joints (friction between the water and the pipe walls); the warm pump casing and warm water then transfer the heat energy to the surrounding air by conduction and convection (and the pump&rsquo;s noise carries sound energy to the air), until it spreads so widely it can no longer be recovered.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 14, title: "Practical: measuring a person's useful power",
content: `
<p>The power equation on page 8 is not just for kettles &mdash; it works on people, and you can measure your own useful power output with nothing but a staircase and three pieces of equipment. This investigation is a classic of the physics unit because it combines an energy calculation, a power calculation and honest thinking about accuracy.</p>
<p><strong>The idea.</strong> Climbing stairs raises your body against gravity, storing gravitational potential energy in you: E<sub>p</sub> = mgh. If you time the climb, your useful power output is P = E<sub>p</sub> &divide; t. That is genuinely <em>your</em> power &mdash; the rate at which you did useful work on your own body.</p>
<p><strong>Apparatus.</strong> Bathroom scales (to measure mass, in kg); a tape measure or metre stick (to measure the vertical height of the staircase, in m); a stopwatch (to time the climb, in s).</p>
<p><strong>Method.</strong></p>
<p>1. Measure your mass on the scales, and the vertical height from the floor of the stairwell to the top step. To find the total height of several flights, measure the height of one flight&rsquo;s steps and multiply, or measure each flight and add them.</p>
<p>2. Record the height, and stand at the bottom of the stairs. A partner holds the stopwatch.</p>
<p>3. On the signal, climb steadily &mdash; do not sprint and do not pause &mdash; while your partner times from your first moving foot to your last foot landing on the top step.</p>
<p>4. Repeat the climb three times, resting in between, and calculate the mean time. Using a mean reduces the effect of random timing variation.</p>
<p>5. Calculate E<sub>p</sub> = m &times; g &times; h, then P = E<sub>p</sub> &divide; t using the mean time.</p>
<h3>Worked example: sample results</h3>
<div style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.6rem;padding:0.8rem 1.1rem;background:#f8fafc;margin:1.1rem 0;">
<p style="margin:0 0 0.5rem;"><strong>The results.</strong> Mass = 55&nbsp;kg; vertical height = 3.6&nbsp;m; times for three climbs = 4.9&nbsp;s, 5.3&nbsp;s, 5.1&nbsp;s.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 1 &mdash; mean time.</strong> (4.9 + 5.3 + 5.1) &divide; 3 = 5.1&nbsp;s.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 2 &mdash; energy.</strong> E<sub>p</sub> = 55 &times; 10 &times; 3.6 = 1980&nbsp;J.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 3 &mdash; power.</strong> P = 1980 &divide; 5.1 = 388&nbsp;W, which rounds to about 390&nbsp;W of useful power.</p>
</div>
<p><strong>Accuracy and limitations.</strong> The stopwatch is the weakest instrument: human reaction time is about 0.2&nbsp;s, which on a 5&nbsp;s climb is a 4&nbsp;% uncertainty &mdash; hence the repeats and mean. The height measurement is strong if taken carefully. And the calculated power is a <em>lower</em> estimate of what the body actually produced: the muscles also transferred energy to heat inside the body, to moving limbs, and to friction, none of which is stored as gravitational potential energy &mdash; which is exactly the useful/wasted distinction of page 11, measured on yourself. A sprinted climb raises the power figure but tires the climber; a slow, steady climb measures sustainable output.</p>
<div style="border-left:4px solid #b45309;background:#fffbeb;padding:0.8rem 1rem;margin:1.2rem 0;border-radius:0.4rem;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>Safety.</strong> Climb at a controlled pace, using the handrail if provided, and check that the staircase is clear. Sensible footwear only &mdash; the risk in this practical is a slip, not the electricity.</p>
</div>
<h3>Practice</h3>
<p>(a) A student records mass 62&nbsp;kg, height 4.0&nbsp;m, mean time 5.5&nbsp;s. Calculate the useful power. (b) Explain why calculating P from a single timed run is less reliable than from the mean of three runs. (c) The student sprints the climb and obtains a much higher power figure. State whether the higher figure is wrong, and explain the physics behind the difference.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) E<sub>p</sub> = 62 &times; 10 &times; 4.0 = 2480&nbsp;J; P = 2480 &divide; 5.5 = 451&nbsp;W, about 450&nbsp;W. (b) A single timing carries the full effect of random errors &mdash; reaction-time delay at both the start and the stop, plus any stumble or hesitation &mdash; and could be high or low by chance; averaging three runs shares out that random variation, giving a value closer to the true time. (c) The higher figure is not wrong: the same energy was transferred in a shorter time, so the power genuinely was greater (P = E &divide; t). What changes is what the number means &mdash; the sprint measures short-burst output, which the body can sustain by drawing on its chemical energy stores, while the steady climb measures sustainable output; neither is a truer measurement, they are measurements of two different things.</p></div></details>` },

{ order: 15, title: "Chapter review: the energy toolkit",
content: `<p>Step back and this chapter is a small, complete toolkit: one great law, three equations, and a way of thinking about every device you will ever meet. Here is the whole set, gathered in one place.</p>
<p><strong>The law.</strong> Conservation of energy: energy is never created or destroyed, only transferred between forms and places. Every equation in this chapter is the law applied to a particular situation, and every &ldquo;missing&rdquo; joule is somewhere &mdash; usually warming the surroundings.</p>
<p><strong>The forms.</strong> Nine of them &mdash; chemical, heat, electrical, sound, light, magnetic, strain, kinetic and gravitational potential &mdash; and every device is a story of energy moving between them. The <strong>useful</strong> output is what the device is for; everything else is <strong>wasted</strong>, chiefly as heat and sound.</p>
<p><strong>The equations.</strong> E<sub>k</sub> = &frac12;mv&sup2; for moving objects; E<sub>p</sub> = mgh (with g = 10&nbsp;N/kg, measuring h vertically from a stated level) for raised ones; P = E &divide; t for the rate of transfer, in watts; and efficiency = (useful &divide; total) &times; 100&nbsp;%. Where two forms exchange energy &mdash; a falling object, a swing, a lift &mdash; conservation of energy supplies the connecting equation: energy lost from one form equals energy gained by the other.</p>
<figure style="margin:1.4rem 0;text-align:center;"><img src="/lesson-assets/sas-physics/sas-phys-sankey-lamp.png" alt="The Sankey diagram of the filament lamp: 100 joules of electrical energy in, 10 joules of useful light out and 90 joules of wasted heat out, showing the widths in proportion to the amounts." style="width:100%;max-width:100%;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.75rem;background:#fff;"><figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem;font-size:0.9rem;color:#475569;text-align:left;">The chapter in one picture. A Sankey diagram shows conservation (the widths add up), useful versus wasted energy (the split), and efficiency (the ratio of the bands) &mdash; the three ideas this whole chapter has been building. Diagram drawn for this course.</figcaption></figure>
<h3>Worked example: planning a full-mark answer</h3>
<div style="border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.6rem;padding:0.8rem 1.1rem;background:#f8fafc;margin:1.1rem 0;">
<p style="margin:0 0 0.5rem;"><strong>The question.</strong> &ldquo;A hydroelectric power station uses falling water to generate electricity. Describe the energy transfers involved, from stored water to homes, and explain why the station cannot be 100&nbsp;% efficient.&rdquo; Notice the command words: <em>describe</em> the transfers (a chain, in order), then <em>explain</em> the losses (reasons, not a list).</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 1 &mdash; name the starting store.</strong> Water held high in a reservoir stores gravitational potential energy (E<sub>p</sub> = mgh for the mass of water and the drop).</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 2 &mdash; trace the chain.</strong> Falling, the water gains kinetic energy; it spins a turbine, transferring the kinetic energy to the generator, which converts it into electrical energy; the grid carries it to homes, where appliances convert it yet again (a kettle into heat, a lamp into light).</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0;"><strong>Step 3 &mdash; account for the losses.</strong> At each stage some energy is wasted: friction in the pipes warms the water; friction in the turbine and generator warms the machinery and produces sound; resistance in the cables warms them. By conservation of energy the wasted joules still exist &mdash; they have simply spread into the surroundings as heat.</p>
<p style="margin:0.4rem 0 0;"><strong>Step 4 &mdash; answer the &ldquo;why not 100&nbsp;%&rdquo; directly.</strong> Because friction and electrical resistance are unavoidable in any real machine with moving parts and currents, some energy is always transferred to heat and sound that cannot be recovered &mdash; so useful output must always be less than input, and efficiency must be below 100&nbsp;%.</p>
</div>
<h3>Exam-style practice</h3>
<p>(a) A drone of mass 2&nbsp;kg hovers at a constant height. State the energy transfers happening in the drone every second it hovers, and explain why hovering does not store any gravitational potential energy <em>gain</em>. (b) The drone&rsquo;s battery supplies 300&nbsp;J every second to its motors, which convert 180&nbsp;J of this to useful kinetic energy of the spinning rotors. Calculate the efficiency of the motors. (c) A wind turbine is 45&nbsp;% efficient. Its generator takes in energy from the wind at a rate of 200&nbsp;kW. Calculate the useful electrical power output, and state one reason why the remaining 55&nbsp;% is considered wasted. (d) Explain, using ideas from this chapter, why electric cars are described as more efficient than petrol cars.</p>
<details style="margin:0.6rem 0 1.1rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:0.5rem;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;background:#f8fafc;"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#1d4ed8;">Show the answer</summary><div style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><p>(a) The battery&rsquo;s chemical energy is transferred to electrical energy by the circuit, then to kinetic energy of the spinning rotors (which pushes air downwards to hold the drone up), and to heat energy in the motors and sound energy &mdash; the rotor&rsquo;s kinetic energy is itself immediately transferred to the moving air. At constant height the drone gains no gravitational potential energy because h is not changing; all the input energy flows straight through to the outputs. (b) Efficiency = (180 &divide; 300) &times; 100&nbsp;% = 60&nbsp;%. (c) Useful output = 45&nbsp;% of 200&nbsp;kW = 0.45 &times; 200 = 90&nbsp;kW; the rest is wasted because it is transferred to forms no one wanted &mdash; heat in the generator and gearbox, sound of the blades, and kinetic energy left in the air flowing past the turbine, all spreading into the surroundings where it cannot be recovered. (d) A petrol car engine is only about 25&ndash;30&nbsp;% efficient: most of the fuel&rsquo;s chemical energy leaves as heat through the exhaust and radiator; electric motors convert a much larger fraction of their input energy (often over 80&nbsp;%) into useful kinetic energy, wasting far less as heat, so more of the energy supplied actually moves the car.</p></div></details>` },
];